<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Hardened-runtime entitlements for Coincube. Applied at codesign
        time by `rcodesign sign`, pointed here with its entitlements XML
        path flag (or by `codesign` with its entitlements flag). See
        contrib/release/macos/README.md for the full codesign and
        notarize workflow.

        NEVER write a double hyphen inside these comments, not even inside
        a flag name. XML forbids it, and while `plutil` and rcodesign's
        parser both accept it, Apple's AMFI parser does not: `codesign`
        rejects the whole file with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error"
        and signs nothing. A flag name written out in full cost us
        exactly that, and the failure names a line number in a comment,
        which reads like a corrupt file rather than a prose typo.

        Network entitlements are required for the local LAN signer
        (`phone_signer` module): we open a TCP listener during pairing,
        we dial the phone's TLS endpoint at steady state, and we
        multicast for the mDNS browse/advertise.
    -->

    <!-- Accept inbound TCP connections (pairing listener). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Dial outbound TCP connections (PhoneSigner steady-state). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
        Data-protection keychain access group for the device secret.

        The value is `<TeamID>.<bundleID>` and must stay in sync with
        CFBundleIdentifier in the Info.plist baked into `_coincube.zip`
        (io.coincube.desktop). Team ID 8UVR249AD5 is written literally on
        purpose: `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` and `$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)` are
        Xcode build-setting substitutions, and `rcodesign` signs this file
        verbatim without expanding anything — a signature carrying the raw
        variable string fails at runtime looking exactly like a keychain bug.

        The device-secret code does not set kSecAttrAccessGroup, so new items
        land in the FIRST group listed here, and reads search every listed
        group. An access group is part of a keychain item's identity: once a
        signed, entitled build has shipped, dropping a group strands every
        item written under it.

        The second group is the legacy Tenshu one (bundle ID
        io.coincube.tenshu, used by nightly and local builds from 2026-08 to
        2026-11). It stays listed so the app can still read the device
        secrets and imported-inventory anchors those builds wrote, and copy
        them forward (services/unlock/device_secret/mod.rs,
        services/imported_inventory_anchor/apple.rs). The profile grants the
        team wildcard 8UVR249AD5.*, which covers both. Keep the new group
        first, and do not remove the legacy one without a decision that
        Tenshu-era installs no longer need to open.
    -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>8UVR249AD5.io.coincube.desktop</string>
        <string>8UVR249AD5.io.coincube.tenshu</string>
    </array>

    <!--
        Application identity, required by AuthenticationServices.

        Without this key, every WebAuthn registration and assertion fails with
        ASAuthorizationError 1004, "The calling process does not have an
        application identifier". Signing, notarization, spctl and the keychain
        entitlement are all unaffected and all pass, and launching through
        LaunchServices rather than exec makes no difference: the framework looks
        for this entitlement in the signature and finds nothing.

        The embedded provisioning profile has always GRANTED
        com.apple.application-identifier, but a profile only authorises an
        entitlement. It has to be requested here too or the signature simply
        does not carry it, which is the state every build was in before
        2026-08-07. That is why the passkey path could never have worked on a
        shipped artifact, only on a developer machine that never got far enough
        to notice.

        Unlike keychain-access-groups (granted as the team wildcard
        8UVR249AD5.*) and associated-domains (granted as *), this value must
        match the profile EXACTLY. It is TeamID.bundleID, written literally for
        the same reason the access group is: nothing expands Xcode's
        $(AppIdentifierPrefix) here.

        macOS uses the com.apple.application-identifier spelling. The bare
        application-identifier key is the iOS form and is ignored here, so a
        signature carrying only that one fails exactly as if the key were
        absent.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
    <string>8UVR249AD5.io.coincube.desktop</string>

    <!--
        Team identity. Granted by the profile alongside the application
        identifier, and expected next to it by the frameworks that read one.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
    <string>8UVR249AD5</string>

    <!--
        WebAuthn relying party for macOS passkey unlock. Also restricted, and
        also authorised by the embedded provisioning profile (which grants
        `com.apple.developer.associated-domains` as a wildcard).

        The domain must match the AASA served from coincube.io, whose
        `webcredentials.apps` array must list
        8UVR249AD5.io.coincube.desktop (keep the Tenshu ID there during the
        transition), and the RP ID the app passes to
        AuthenticationServices (`COINCUBE_PASSKEY_RP_ID`, which still defaults
        to "localhost" — see services/passkey/mod.rs).

        Inert until COINCUBE_ENABLE_PASSKEY is on: nothing requests a passkey
        ceremony today, so this only makes the capability available to a build
        that does.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
    <array>
        <string>webcredentials:coincube.io</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
